        },
        {
          "id": "action_HyLceV9_",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Alright, let’s dive into how we structure text on a web page. This slide is all about headings, paragraphs, and best practices for formatting."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_bJFcx5L2",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_dCRLGSye"
        },
        {
          "id": "action__1ZOeaq4",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "First, the <h1> through <h6> tags create a hierarchy of headings. <h1> is the top‑level title, <h2> is a sub‑heading, and so on down to <h6>."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_uDtXiHV-",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_dCRLGSye"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_EGlLjrcQ",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Next, the <p> tag defines a paragraph block. Whenever you need a separate chunk of text, wrap it in <p>…</p> and the browser will add spacing automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_SxHlBByd",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_dCRLGSye"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_614A4hh_",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "A key best practice is to use only one <h1> per page—think of it as the main headline. All other headings should be <h2> or lower, preserving a clear outline for both readers and search engines."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_YPizR5Z3",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "To recap, we have a heading hierarchy with <h1>–<h6>, paragraphs created with <p>, and the rule of a single <h1> per page. These simple steps keep your HTML clean and easy to navigate."
        }
      ],
      "createdAt": 1777632861174,
      "updatedAt": 1777632861174
    },
    {
      "id": "scene_fyxnpkXOpd",
      "stageId": "NDywxqJaL7",
      "type": "slide",
      "title": "Links and Images",
      "order": 5,
      "content": {
        "type": "slide",
        "canvas": {
          "id": "dlANLxtk9d9n8QGuoyH8q",
          "viewportSize": 1000,
          "viewportRatio": 0.5625,
          "theme": {
            "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
            "themeColors": [
              "#5b9bd5",
              "#ed7d31",
              "#a5a5a5",
              "#ffc000",
              "#4472c4"
            ],
            "fontColor": "#333333",
            "fontName": "Microsoft YaHei",
            "outline": {
              "color": "#d14424",
              "width": 2,
              "style": "solid"
            },
            "shadow": {
              "h": 0,
              "v": 0,
              "blur": 10,
              "color": "#000000"
            }
          },
          "elements": [
            {
              "id": "text_rOp1y4Ic",
              "type": "text",
              "left": 60,
              "top": 50,
              "width": 880,
              "height": 58,
              "content": "<p style=\"font-size:32px;\"><strong>Links and Images</strong></p>",
              "defaultFontName": "Microsoft YaHei",
              "defaultColor": "#333333",
              "rotate": 0
            },
            {
              "id": "text_oS_KFgAv",
              "type": "text",
              "left": 60,
              "top": 130,
              "width": 880,
              "height": 82,
              "content": "<p style=\"font-size:20px;\">• <code>&lt;a href=\"...\"&gt;</code> creates a hyperlink</p><p style=\"font-size:20px;\">• <code>&lt;img src=\"...\" alt=\"...\"&gt;</code> inserts an image</p><p style=\"font-size:20px;\">• Use descriptive <code>alt</code> text for accessibility</p>",
              "defaultFontName": "Microsoft YaHei",
              "defaultColor": "#333333",
              "rotate": 0
            }
          ],
          "background": {
            "type": "solid",
            "color": "#ffffff"
          }
        }
      },
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_gwqvSl_Y",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_rOp1y4Ic"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_dDNtAvIB",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "All right, let’s dive into how we add links and images to our web pages."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_N8XXl_PZ",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_oS_KFgAv"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_EWA-7t9T",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "First, the anchor tag &lt;a href=\"...\"&gt; creates a hyperlink. The href attribute points to the destination URL, and the text between the opening and closing tags becomes the clickable link."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_CK_whj5J",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_oS_KFgAv"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_J7n_K0-o",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "For example, &lt;a href=\"https://example.com\"&gt;Visit Example&lt;/a&gt; will display the word “Visit Example” as a link that opens the page when clicked."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_ZIUj88Nq",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_oS_KFgAv"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_s9M6ZAQd",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Next, the image tag &lt;img src=\"...\" alt=\"...\"&gt; inserts a picture. The src attribute tells the browser where to find the image file, and the alt attribute provides descriptive text for accessibility."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_iWSk4kIW",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_oS_KFgAv"
        },
        {
          "id": "action__OFeArz7",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "For instance, &lt;img src=\"cat.jpg\" alt=\"A playful orange tabby cat\"&gt; will show the cat picture, and screen readers will read the alt text for users who can’t see the image."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_3-9q1RHD",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_oS_KFgAv"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_bWiBERdB",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Remember, using clear, descriptive alt text makes our pages more inclusive for everyone."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_08QKhS3L",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "That covers the basics of adding navigation links and visual media. Any questions before we move on?"
        }
      ],
      "createdAt": 1777632895572,
      "updatedAt": 1777632895572
    },
    {
      "id": "scene_krKif4YulR",
      "stageId": "NDywxqJaL7",
      "type": "slide",
      "title": "Lists, Tables, and Forms",
      "order": 6,
      "content": {
        "type": "slide",
        "canvas": {
          "id": "3d1w_COg9wcHuYVdZ-hvf",
          "viewportSize": 1000,
          "viewportRatio": 0.5625,
          "theme": {
            "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
            "themeColors": [
              "#5b9bd5",
              "#ed7d31",
              "#a5a5a5",
              "#ffc000",
              "#4472c4"
            ],
            "fontColor": "#333333",
            "fontName": "Microsoft YaHei",
            "outline": {
              "color": "#d14424",
              "width": 2,
              "style": "solid"
            },
            "shadow": {
              "h": 0,
              "v": 0,
              "blur": 10,
              "color": "#000000"
            }
          },
          "elements": [
            {
              "id": "text_SvU8aIrW",
              "type": "text",
              "left": 60,
              "top": 50,
              "width": 880,
              "height": 70,
              "content": "<p style=\"font-size:32px;\"><strong>Lists, Tables, and Forms</strong></p>",
              "defaultFontName": "Microsoft YaHei",
              "defaultColor": "#333333",
              "rotate": 0
            },
            {
              "id": "text_zB8HYbox",
              "type": "text",
              "left": 60,
              "top": 140,
              "width": 880,
              "height": 103,
              "content": "<p style=\"font-size:18px;\">• Ordered (<code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>) vs. unordered (<code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>) lists</p><p style=\"font-size:18px;\">• Table layout with <code>&lt;table&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;th&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;td&gt;</code></p><p style=\"font-size:18px;\">• Basic form elements: <code>&lt;form&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;input&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;button&gt;</code></p>",
              "defaultFontName": "Microsoft YaHei",
              "defaultColor": "#333333",
              "rotate": 0
            },
            {
              "id": "text_PviPQNVt",
              "type": "text",
              "left": 60,
              "top": 260,
              "width": 880,
              "height": 130,
              "content": "<p style=\"font-size:16px; font-family:monospace;\">&lt;!-- List example --&gt;<br/>&lt;ul&gt;<br/>  &lt;li&gt;Item 1&lt;/li&gt;<br/>  &lt;li&gt;Item 2&lt;/li&gt;<br/>&lt;/ul&gt;<br/><br/>&lt;!-- Table example --&gt;<br/>&lt;table&gt;<br/>  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Header&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;<br/>  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Data&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;<br/>&lt;/table&gt;<br/><br/>&lt;!-- Form example --&gt;<br/>&lt;form&gt;<br/>  &lt;label&gt;Name:&lt;/label&gt; &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; /&gt;<br/>  &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Send&lt;/button&gt;<br/>&lt;/form&gt;</p>",
              "defaultFontName": "Microsoft YaHei",
              "defaultColor": "#333333",
              "rotate": 0
            }
          ],
          "background": {
            "type": "solid",
            "color": "#ffffff"
          }
        }
      },
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_9Thwv1ye",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_SvU8aIrW"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_FNqQrYBx",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Alright, let’s dive into Lists, Tables, and Forms, which are the building blocks for organizing content and collecting user input on a web page."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_gNnE1Amj",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_zB8HYbox"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_s81rR06Q",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "First, notice the difference between ordered lists, marked up with <ol>, and unordered lists, marked up with <ul>. Ordered lists number each item, while unordered lists use bullets."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_XGg7cwAW",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_PviPQNVt"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_oQ-yqsy6",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Here’s a quick example of an unordered list in HTML. Each <li> element creates a separate bullet point, just like a shopping list you might write on paper."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_aNlMYHJG",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_zB8HYbox"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_z8c-q3G-",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Next, tables let us display data in rows and columns. We use <table> to start, <tr> for each row, <th> for header cells, and <td> for regular data cells."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_uUsmhDCQ",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_zB8HYbox"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_ExvGR5g1",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Finally, forms are how we gather information from users. The <form> tag wraps input controls like <input>, <label>, <textarea>, and <button>."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_FCdRZZ2e",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "That covers the essentials for today—lists to structure items, tables to present tabular data, and forms to collect input. Great job following along!"
        }
      ],
      "createdAt": 1777632938302,
      "updatedAt": 1777632938302
    },
    {
      "id": "scene_DEzGvkJVnJ",
      "stageId": "NDywxqJaL7",
      "type": "slide",
      "title": "Semantic HTML & Good Practices",
      "order": 7,
      "content": {
        "type": "slide",
        "canvas": {
          "id": "qtHe12raMSN1qoOyq35TK",
          "viewportSize": 1000,
          "viewportRatio": 0.5625,
          "theme": {
            "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
            "themeColors": [
              "#5b9bd5",
              "#ed7d31",
              "#a5a5a5",
              "#ffc000",
              "#4472c4"
            ],
            "fontColor": "#333333",
            "fontName": "Microsoft YaHei",
            "outline": {
              "color": "#d14424",
              "width": 2,
              "style": "solid"
            },
            "shadow": {
              "h": 0,
              "v": 0,
              "blur": 10,
              "color": "#000000"
            }
          },
          "elements": [
            {
              "id": "text_okPZxJSZ",
              "type": "text",
              "left": 60,
              "top": 60,
              "width": 880,
              "height": 58,
              "content": "<p style=\"font-size:32px;\"><strong>Semantic HTML & Good Practices</strong></p>",
              "defaultFontName": "Microsoft YaHei",
              "defaultColor": "#333333",
              "rotate": 0
            },
            {
              "id": "text_XIwAS9E0",
              "type": "text",
              "left": 60,
              "top": 140,
              "width": 880,
              "height": 103,
              "content": "<p style=\"font-size:18px;\">• Semantic tags: <header>, <nav>, <main>, <section>, <article>, <footer></p><p style=\"font-size:18px;\">• Improves accessibility and SEO</p><p style=\"font-size:18px;\">• Indent code, use meaningful headings, add alt text</p>",
              "defaultFontName": "Microsoft YaHei",
              "defaultColor": "#333333",
              "rotate": 0
            }
          ],
          "background": {
            "type": "solid",
            "color": "#ffffff"
          }
        }
      },
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_LR4EK32y",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_okPZxJSZ"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_Ayij1bB8",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Alright everyone, let’s dive into why using semantic HTML matters and how good coding habits can make our pages stronger."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_yWOks_bN",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_XIwAS9E0"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_wU4ebhqR",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "First, semantic tags like <header>, <nav>, <main>, <section>, <article>, and <footer> give meaning to the structure of a page. They tell browsers, screen readers, and search engines what each part of the content represents."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_70bYMuHx",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_XIwAS9E0"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_uVuPq8F0",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Because of that meaning, accessibility improves—screen readers can navigate more easily, and SEO benefits because search engines understand the hierarchy of your content."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_9lxp1_uG",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_XIwAS9E0"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_eOdx8WS-",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Lastly, remember to keep your code tidy: indent properly, use clear headings, and always add alt text to images. Those small habits make your markup both readable and maintainable."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_RBq_vc5o",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Let’s try applying these practices in our next exercise, and feel free to ask any questions as we go."
        }
      ],
      "createdAt": 1777632970159,
      "updatedAt": 1777632970159
    },
    {
      "id": "scene_A9W8j4xu_p",
      "stageId": "NDywxqJaL7",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Knowledge Check",
      "order": 8,
      "content": {
        "type": "quiz",
        "questions": [
          {
            "id": "q1",
            "type": "single",
            "question": "Which HTML tag is used to define the most important heading on a page?",
            "options": [
              {
                "value": "A",
                "label": "<h1>"
              },
              {
                "value": "B",
                "label": "<header>"
              },
              {
                "value": "C",
                "label": "<title>"
              },
              {
                "value": "D",
                "label": "<head>"
              }
            ],
            "answer": [
              "A"
            ],
            "analysis": "<h1> creates the primary heading and is the highest level heading element; <header> is a structural container, <title> belongs in the <head> section, and <head> is not displayed as content.",
            "points": 10,
            "hasAnswer": true
          },
          {
            "id": "q2",
            "type": "multiple",
            "question": "Select all elements that are part of the basic HTML document structure in the correct order (choose all that apply).",
            "options": [
              {
                "value": "A",
                "label": "<!DOCTYPE html>"
              },
              {
                "value": "B",
                "label": "<html>"
              },
              {
                "value": "C",
                "label": "<body>"
              },
              {
                "value": "D",
                "label": "<head>"
              },
              {
                "value": "E",
                "label": "<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">"
              }
            ],
            "answer": [
              "A",
              "B",
              "D",
              "C"
            ],
            "analysis": "A correct order is: <!DOCTYPE html> (declaration), <html> (root), <head> (metadata), then <body> (content). The <meta> tag belongs inside <head> but is not a required top‑level element for the structure ordering question.",
            "points": 15,
            "hasAnswer": true
          },
          {
            "id": "q3",
            "type": "short_answer",
            "question": "You are marking up a blog post that contains a main article, a navigation menu, and a footer with contact info. Which semantic HTML5 element should you use for the main article content?",
            "commentPrompt": "Rubric: (1) Mention of the <article> element - 50%; (2) Explanation why <article> is appropriate for standalone content - 30%; (3) Clarity and conciseness of answer - 20%",
            "analysis": "The correct element is <article>, because it represents a self‑contained composition that could be independently distributed or reused, such as a blog post.",
            "points": 20,
            "hasAnswer": false
          }
        ]
      },
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_zSXLbHpt",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Alright, let’s see how well you’ve grasped the basics of HTML structure. Read each question carefully and choose the best answer."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_ZIQ2ZdUh",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "For the first question, remember that the strongest heading tag is <h1>. It signals the main title of the page and is the most important heading for both readers and search engines."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_0PdDJp5d",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "The next two items focus on the overall document skeleton. The typical order starts with the <!DOCTYPE html> declaration, then the <html> element, followed by the <head> (where you place things like <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">), and finally the <body> where the visible content lives. And when you need to mark up the main article of a blog post, the semantic <article> element is the right choice."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_dSBr5iDM",
          "type": "discussion",
          "topic": "Why is using the correct heading level and semantic elements important for accessibility and SEO?",
          "prompt": "Think about how browsers and assistive technologies interpret these tags.",
          "agentId": "gen-server-3"
        }
      ],
      "createdAt": 1777633003852,
      "updatedAt": 1777633003852
    }
  ],
  "scenesCount": 8,
